 ## Subtopics
 
-### Anisotropic SPH Kernel
-An extension of the standard SPH kernel that uses an anisotropy matrix $\mathbf{H}$ to create an ellipsoidal support domain, addressing issues with fluid surface rendering where isotropic support leads to artifacts [S2]. It employs a symmetric formulation averaging the kernel evaluation of two interacting particles to ensure consistency [S2]. It is used specifically for fluid surface rendering, while the simulation itself may remain isotropic [S2].
+### [Anisotropic SPH Kernel](18763-anisotropic-sph-kernel.md)
+An extension that replaces the spherical support domain with an ellipsoidal one defined by an anisotropy matrix, designed to reduce artifacts in fluid surface rendering [18763-anisotropic-sph-kernel.md]. It differs from the isotropic parent by introducing directional dependence in the footprint to better capture surface geometry [18763-anisotropic-sph-kernel.md]. Choose this variant when rendering fluid interfaces where isotropic kernels produce visible smoothing artifacts. The provided sources do not detail its algorithmic implementation.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per kernel evaluation. Neighbor search complexity depends on the underlying data structure (kd-tree), typically $O(\log N)$ for search [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Low, as kernels are stateless functions. The kd-tree requires $O(N)$ space [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Closed-form derivatives allow for efficient gradient-based operations (e.g., in density calculation or voxelization). Support for multiple kernel types (B-spline, Wendland) provides flexibility for different simulation requirements [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Anisotropic kernels require non-cubic cells in spatial data structures, complicating neighborhood queries [S2]. The source notes a potential ambiguity in the normalization constant for the rendering kernel ($C_3 = \frac{64}{64\pi}$) [S2].
-*   **When to use:** When visualizing SPH simulation data, particularly fluid surfaces, where accurate density estimation or smooth voxelization is required [S1, unofficial][S2].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per kernel evaluation; neighbor search via kd-tree typically requires $O(\log N)$ [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Low, as kernels are stateless functions; the kd-tree requires $O(N)$ auxiliary space [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Closed-form derivatives enable efficient gradient-based operations for density calculation and voxelization [S1, unofficial]. Support for multiple kernel families allows tuning for different simulation characteristics [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Voxelization requires extending the base algorithm to 3D grids [S1, unofficial]. Exact polynomial coefficients for all supported kernels are not documented [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Visualizing particle-based astrophysical or fluid simulations where accurate density estimation and smooth voxelization are required [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The implementation is based on the C++ module defined in [Bös25], providing a polymorphic interface for SPH kernels [S1, unofficial]. The voxelization algorithm extends the method described in [Pri07] to support 3D voxels [S1, unofficial]. The anisotropic formulation for fluid surface rendering is based on [OVSM98] and [YT13], with symmetric gathering/scattering derived from [HK89] [S2].
+The method originates from a C++ module defined in [Bös25] that provides a polymorphic interface for SPH kernels [S1, unofficial]. The voxelization pipeline extends the algorithm described in [Pri07] to support 3D voxels [S1, unofficial]. It is used in astrophysical simulation visualization pipelines to bridge particle data with volume rendering formats [S1, unofficial].
 
